            <span class="cc-tag">The redemption cap</span>
          </h2>
          <p class="cc-guide-desc">Udemy coupons carry two independent kill switches, and hitting either one ends the code instantly. The first is a <strong>time limit</strong>: free open coupons last 5 days, targeted coupons up to 31 days. The second is a <strong>redemption cap</strong> — free coupons allow up to 10 students, targeted coupons up to 100. Most codes die on the cap, not the clock.</p>
          <p class="cc-guide-text"><strong>Why instructors accept that.</strong> A coupon is a launch instrument, not a permanent discount. Instructors mint codes to spike enrollments during a launch window, which drives reviews and lifts the course in Udemy's ranking algorithm. Once the cap fills, the marketing job is done and the course returns to full price — sometimes within a single day for a popular course. That swing is why the same course can sit at $89 on Monday, $12 on Wednesday, and $89 again by Friday.</p>
          <p class="cc-guide-text"><strong>What this site does about it.</strong> Every deal on this page carries a last-checked stamp, and our pipeline re-checks each coupon against Udemy daily. Dead codes are pruned from the listing rather than left to rot — which is why the count on this page moves every day instead of climbing forever. If you are hunting a specific subject, check the stamp before you click through: a coupon verified within the last few hours is far more likely to still be alive than one checked yesterday.</p>
